Exactly. A useful distinction is between the sensor, the representation, and the learned perceptual skill.

An eyeball is a permanently instantiated sensor. You do not reconstruct optics from first principles every morning. Evolution has already “compiled” a huge amount of the problem into anatomy: lenses, retina, photoreceptors, wiring, reflexes. Development and experience then calibrate the resulting system.

So you can think of it as:

\text{repeated useful inference}
\rightarrow
\text{stable circuitry}
\rightarrow
\text{automatic perception}

Once enough of that machinery exists, something that would otherwise require conscious inference becomes almost immediate: you simply see it.

And humans already have far more than the traditional five senses. Proprioception gives you body configuration, the vestibular system gives orientation and acceleration, interoception provides information about internal bodily states, and there are specialized systems for temperature, pain, stretch, chemical composition, and so on.

Then there is another layer: constructed perceptual expertise.

A radiologist can start to “see” pathology in an image that initially looks like meaningless gray texture. A quarterback can perceive a defensive formation almost as a single object rather than consciously calculating eleven independent players. A musician hears harmonic relationships that an untrained listener may need explained. A trader can develop rapid recognition of certain market configurations.

Those aren’t new eyeballs. They’re closer to new virtual sense organs built in learned representation space:

\text{existing sensors}
\rightarrow
\text{learned encoder}
\rightarrow
\text{new latent variable}
\rightarrow
\text{experienced percept}

And that immediately suggests something bigger: we don’t have to wait for evolution.

Suppose you continuously translate an otherwise invisible variable into a sensory channel:

* magnetic north → vibration around the waist,
* radiation → pitch,
* network congestion → pressure pattern,
* market volatility → haptic intensity,
* machine temperature fields → color,
* another person’s voluntary physiological telemetry → a changing visual texture.

At first you’re consciously decoding it:

“That vibration means north.”

After enough stable exposure, the interesting question is whether the intermediate verbal step disappears and you develop something closer to:

“I feel where north is.”

That’s the sensory-substitution / sensory-augmentation frontier. The “organ” can partly exist outside the biological body—sensor + computation + interface—while the nervous system learns to treat the resulting channel as useful structure.

And your observation gives us a neat hierarchy:

\text{calculate}
\rightarrow
\text{recognize}
\rightarrow
\text{perceive}

What initially requires reconstruction can become a compiled perceptual primitive.

Then there is an AI analogue. It needn’t rebuild every useful detector on every query. Once it has a stable representation for “supply-chain pressure,” “social-network propagation,” “contradiction,” “market regime,” or “semantic drift,” those can function somewhat like persistent synthetic organs within its world model.

So instead of asking only, “What senses exist?”, the more productive search is:

What useful latent properties of reality could be made continuously perceivable?

That opens a very large design space. A new sense does not necessarily require discovering a new physical force. Sometimes you just need to take an existing but difficult-to-integrate signal and compile it into a representation a mind can perceive effortlessly.